k and in all honesty  I think it says there was a meltdown in  the  the meltdown was from the others not  wanting to put their name next to a vote  it certainly wasn't from me asking for  one  yeah I'm keen to kind of talk about this  because it does further some of the  briefings were pretty unusual shall we  say around new and according to reports  in the press your behavior was described  as hysterical and aggressive as you say  people said you had a meltdown in  October an unnamed Treasury saw said of  you the only thing she knows how to do  well it's a blow dryer is it sexist well  look different people use the media in  different ways to portray a story and to  get an issue out there there was two  things that I wanted to deliver and that  was a brexit that the UK voted for but  unfortunately in the cabinet the  majority of people and those in the key  posts all voted remain from the prime  minister to the Chancellor to the Deputy  Prime Minister to the Home Secretary to  the foreign secretary so obviously  that's obviously an issue there and the  other thing that I wants to do was get  more money for universal credit because  yes it does a heck of a lot of good but  for the most vulnerable it wasn't  supporting so I succeeded in getting in  the budget four and a half billion  pounds extra and again at the time  everybody didn't want that to happen  either so that could be why you've had  briefings because it was on to issues  more money for universal credit and to  to deliver the brexit vote that the  people of the UK put forward okay should  we deal with universal credit because it  feels as if and there's been a lot  written about it people with feelings  from very very strongly about universal  credit and and I know that that's  something that course you were  responsible for up to your resignation  now according to the traceur Trust and  they say on average twelve months after  universal credit is rolled out food  banks see a 52% increase in demand 70%  of recipients find themselves in debt  while waiting for their payments and  only eight percent say that payment  covers their cost of living I mean is  that a record you're proud of  well the Trussell trust there you've got  to look at where the source is coming  from who have been interviewed to give  those comments and notes are from people  coming into the Trostle trust so those  results weren't  authenticated they weren't sort of  verified as such people were in hardship  when they had to wait for those payment  but come through what I will say is and  that's what I spent eight months doing  saying okay how do we sort out one sort  of extreme of people saying it isn't  working at all two other people are  saying it's the best thing that ever  happened  so for 85% of people or more they are  saying it's helping us into work we're  working more than ever before which  we've seen a thousand people every day  each and every day since 2010 are in  work so it's working there but what I  looked at is our but for the people it's  not working for so those are the people  who will find themselves going into the  Trussell trust social people will find  themselves going to our support groups  and budget groups the Christians against  poverty CA the various other  institutions so I met with all them and  said what can we do  hence I thought for the extra four and a  half billion in the budget hence I  fought for one and a half billion pounds  to make sure that the severe disability  premium was there for half a million  extra people so I said how do we make it  work  what money needs to come forward but not  just money it was about how it was  delivered to so people would say how can  we make sure that the payment period and  the assessment period doesn't knock out  our payment how do we make sure that we  get money straight away which you can  get with in advance and going forward  how do we make sure it's not always paid  monthly  it could be paid weekly fortnightly  three