y as she's always said no  deal is better than a bad deal that's  what we said that's what we agreed in  article 50 is there and we are coming  out of the EU I should apply to me in a  in a Prime Minister's Questions we're  coming out on the 29th of March so  though it's better than the deal on the  table that's what you're saying we've  always said if we cannot come to an  agreement then we will be coming out  without one so we've always said no deal  was better than a bad deal not remain  remain was never there and the people  voted for out so what she's got to say  cuz the EU doesn't want no deal we don't  want no deal so between us of course we  can get a better deal for the UK  backstop goes the 39 billion is for a  future agreement not to talk about an  agreement  who'd paid 39 billions to talk about an  agreement 39 billion is for that  agreement at the same time there's been  some pretty concerning warnings about  the impact of no deal and I know there's  a lot of skepticism about some of the  forecasts but it does feel as if there  is perhaps a you know a unanimous  recognition that there will be an  economic  hit if there is no deal is that a price  worth penny we've heard project fair one  when we heard all of these and coming  back to my department we got told if we  even voted for the referendum half a  million more people would be unemployed  that did not happen we've still got  record rates of employment in fact the  UK has created since 2010 more jobs than  France Spain Ireland Austria Sweden the  Netherlands and Norway put together so  I've heard the project fear stories and  this is yet more of course we can  succeed and of course we want to make  sure that it is a managed a process so  if there is no deal we would have as  we've got now an implementation period  by which we would work together we could  even say he is half the money up front  and then Harvard about there is no  golden period no but why not of course  you could arrange to have one 